00:00:02.909 --> 00:00:04.950 align:start position:0%
 
what's the matter with San Francisco  what's the matter with California what's  the matter with Democrats in this state  the rank and file don't realize what the  folks they're voting for are doing with  that power it has very little to do with  social justice and everything to do with  protecting special interests who don't  give a rat's ass about the poor  by-and-large  I'm Chris Reid I'm an editorial writer  for the San Diego union-tribune and I  also host a radio show part time on KOCO  600 a.m. in San Diego what's going on in  California is astonishing in that it  represents essentially a repudiation of  democratic ideals the Democrats dominate  the state but they're supposed to be  originally all about social justice or  at least since the New Deal in the 1930s  the Democrats are supposed to be about  protecting the poor people the  downtrodden those who can't help  themselves but on issue after issue the  Democratic Party in California uses its  power in ways that hurt the poor and  hurt the downtrodden especially the  minorities that they supposedly champion  the worst single story to appear last  year in the pages of the Los Angeles  Times had to do with proposal by some  parents in a South Los Angeles district  to set up a charter school they were  really unhappy with their school under  state laws countenance by Democrats  poorer schools almost always end up with  very inexperienced teachers who are  often subject to to turnover and so you  have a discontinuity on the faculty and  you have a you know a not a ripe setting  for education reform there's no Jaime  Escalante at this schools so anyway the  parents proposed setting up a charter  school in South LA and what happens  anonymous leaflets are deposited at the  site where they want to have the meeting  to organize for the charter school that  warns that people who come risk  deportation now nobody knows who did  this but there's only one suspect  members of the United Teachers Los  Angeles nobody else has a motive to try  to sandbag charter schools and nobody  else has such a hatred of charter  schools that they would actually  threaten Latinos that if you're an  illegal immigrant are you sure that this  is something you want to do every summer  sometimes stretching into the fall and  weather times are good or bad the  low-hanging fruit are always cuts in  programs for social services I did some  research on this from 2002 to 2007 this  is a period when revenue in California  was exploding because of the housing  boom probably a 1/3 or more increase in  state revenue over that period the  budget for the state Department of  Social Services which handles child and  family welfare programs aid to the blind  and deaf food stamps and more it went up  from 8.2 billion to nine B about the  rate of inflation meanwhile the budget  for schools up 42% and prisons up 88 %  in other words teachers and prison  guards this is social justice doesn't  look that way to me  another example the Democrats offer of  how they are sticking up for poor  people's interests is perverse it has to  do with their opposition to  implementation of the 1996 federal  welfare reform act California has  something like one-third of the nation's  welfare clients and 1/6 of the  population why because California has  refused to implement welfare reform now  welfare reform is broadly described by  many folks across the spectrum as one of  the great anti-poverty programs of  modern American history because it  really did forced millions of people to  change their lifestyles and actively  seek work so the perverse thing is the  Democrats are bagged are bragging about  sandbagging and anti-poverty program but  it gets even worse than that I have seen  anecdotal evidence that one of the  reasons that they may be opposing  welfare reform or sandbagging it is  because they want to protect government  jobs there used to be a lawmaker from  the San Fernando Valley a very  thoughtful and unusual guy moderate  Republican doctor named Keith Richmond  he didn't fit the cookie quarter mold of  Democrats and Republicans on either side  of the size of this ideological divide  he really believed that government  health care should be made available to  poor people to minorities because it's  there it's budgeted it's just  underutilized it's something like a  third to a half of kids who are eligible  haven't been signing up so he proposed  setting up an online system to register  kids for free California health care  programs and he was told forget about it  because because it could lead to a loss  of jobs at the county offices were folks  register for health care for their kids  you can't make this stuff up the idea  that Walmart is the enemy of poor people  is just bizarre on its face Jason Furman  who is now an economist who works for  President Obama used to work for John  Kerry before that worked for Bill  Clinton in 2005 wrote that Walmart is in  arguably a progressive force because  raising folks standard of living helps  people especially poor people but in San  Diego as in throughout California  Democrats have lined up behind the idea  that cheap groceries are a social evil  what what does that have to do with  social justice keeping people from  making ends meet Walmart a single  does more to help social justice than a  dozen Democratic lawmakers well the  rejection of prop 19 means we're going  to see the continuation of the status  quo of the drug war in California and  once again the social justice question  comes to the fore we've had studies as  recently as a few weeks ago that point  out that minorities use marijuana less  than white folks but are  disproportionately punished doesn't that  suggest to social justice advocates that  the drug war isn't exactly you know  helping the people that they supposedly  care about now in the old days the  notion was well Democrats were a little  better on this issue than Republicans  nowadays there's not a dimes worth of  difference between the two i sat in this  very office grilled Jerry Brown a couple  times about the drug war and he would  have none of it  forty years into the drug war when do we  finally have you know our paradigm shift  our epiphany and acknowledge that it's  been a failure well in California the  Democrats were part of the opposition to  prop 19 they were part of the ones who  said we've got to keep with the status  quo how long does the status quo have to  be a complete flop before you give up on  it  the disconnect between the interests of  environmentalists and the interests of  the working class lower socioeconomic  groups in California is immense for  example the Central Valley we have 40%  unemployment now in parts of the Central  Valley and that's primarily Latino  farmworkers because of what is fairly  described as man-made droughts  environmental rules that prevent water  from getting to farms and yet we have  spectacles like Sacramento congresswoman  Doris Matsui who grew up on these farms  in the Central Valley opposing efforts  to provide emergency relief now is this  social justice to care more about fish  than about humans about the farm workers  I don't think so in San Diego you see  minority business after minority  business smaller and less capitalized  basically either having to shut down  most their operations or to lose a great  portion of their workforce or just to  shut down completely because of airboard  rules meant to cut down on diesel  pollution now this is not social justice  to come up with rules that uniquely  punish  really and uniquely put out of business  the less capitalized firms in effect  it's a sopped a big business by  eliminating their smaller competitors by  having these incredibly onerous and  costly new regulations in California  views itself as the leading edge of  environmentalism in the world and maybe  30 or 40 years ago that made sense when  they're fighting for safer cars but at  this point they're fighting for  environmental rules that only make it  more and more difficult to find jobs in  category after category and the the  progress that's being made on air and  water pollution is marginal it's not the  primary stuff anymore on the margins  they're fighting in ways that hurt  people day after day after day the ab32  is the state's landmark anti-global  warming law passed in 2006 forcing a  switch to cleaner but costlier energy  now apart from the fact that it's crazy  for a single state to go unilaterally  and do what it would take a worldwide  effort to make substantial gains in the  fact is is that this is just an assault  on poor people it's going to force up  the cost of retail energy by 40 to 60  percent and so California which already  has 12 percent unemployment will have a  uniquely uncompetitive business  environment we're already 51st out of 50  states according to the CEOs of America  they also included Washington DC and now  we're going to add on top of that retail  energy costs that are 40 to 60 percent  higher new mandates on businesses that  they have to do a variety of things to  comply with these anti greenhouse gas  goals we may look back on the years of  12% unemployment as the good old days  we're all going to suffer but who's  going to suffer the most who is the  least equipped to deal with an economic  downturn poor folks without savings poor  folks without the educational resources  necessarily to find new jobs this is  social justice it's not my idea of  social justice

